@id     07_cpp_hello
@title  Hello, C++
@topic  output
@lang   cpp
@show   values

@theory
C++ grew out of C, so everything you learned still works. It adds easier tools.

    #include <iostream>
    #include <string>

    int main() {
        std::string name = "Ann";
        std::cout << "Hi " << name << "!" << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

std::cout is the screen. The arrows << send each piece to it, from left to right.
std::endl ends the line, like \n in C. std::cin >> x reads a value from the keyboard.
std::string holds text of any length, and + joins two strings.

Your task:
The program already reads a name into `name`. Print a greeting with it:

    Hello, Sam!

- Hello, a comma, a space, the name, then !
- End the line with `std::endl`.

**Hint:** send the pieces one after another with `<<`.

@read
## From C to C++
C++ grew out of C. Almost everything you learned in C still works, but C++ adds tools that make common jobs easier and safer. The first two you will meet are std::cout for output and std::string for text.

    #include <iostream>
    #include <string>

    int main() {
        std::string name = "Ann";
        std::cout << "Hi " << name << "!" << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

## Output with std::cout
- `std::cout` is the screen.
- `<<` sends a value to it. You can chain several: text, numbers and variables, printed from left to right.
- `std::endl` ends the line, like `\n` in C.

No %d or %s is needed: cout knows how to print each type.

## Input with std::cin
`std::cin >> x` reads a value typed on the keyboard into x. The arrows show which way the data moves: out to the screen with <<, in from the keyboard with >>.

    int age;
    std::cin >> age;

## Text with std::string
A std::string holds text of any length. `+` joins two strings, and `.size()` tells how many characters there are.

    std::string first = "Code";
    std::string both = first + "Lens";    // "CodeLens"

## Read more
learncpp.com, "Introduction to iostream: cout, cin, and endl" and lesson 5.7 "Introduction to std::string"

@starter
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name;
    std::cin >> name;

    // print Hello, <name>!

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name;
    std::cin >> name;

    std::cout << "Hello, " << name << "!" << std::endl;

    return 0;
}

@test
@input
Sam
@expected
Hello, Sam!

@test
@input
Ann
@expected
Hello, Ann!

@hidden_test
@input
Codelens
@expected
Hello, Codelens!

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step watch
@title  Watch cout
@code
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name = "Ann";
    int age = 20;
    std::cout << "Hi " << name << "!" << std::endl;
    age = age + 1;
    std::cout << name << " is now " << age << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch what appears in the program output.
std::cout << sends each piece to the screen, left to right. std::endl ends the line.

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hi ";
    std::cout << "there" << std::endl;
    std::cout << 2 + 3 << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
@text
What does this program print?
The first line has no std::endl, so the next text continues on the same line. 2 + 3 is calculated before it is printed.

@step choose
@title  Join two strings
@code
std::string first = "Code";
std::string second = "Lens";
std::string both = first + second;
@text
What is in both after these lines run?
@option "Code Lens"
@option "CodeLens"
@option "Code+Lens"
@option An error: you cannot add text
@correct 2
@answer
+ joins two std::string values with nothing in between, so both holds "CodeLens".

@step order
@title  Put the program in order
@text
Put the lines in order so the program reads a name and greets it.
A variable must exist before cin can read into it, and before cout can print it.
@code
std::string name;
std::cin >> name;
std::cout << "Hi " << name << std::endl;

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix line 6 so the program prints Hello, Ann! on the screen.
Right now the program does not even compile. Compare line 6 with the cout lines you watched in step 1.
@starter
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name = "Ann";
    std::cout >> "Hello, " >> name >> "!" >> std::endl;
    return 0;
}
@editable 6-6
@solution
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string name = "Ann";
    std::cout << "Hello, " << name << "!" << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
@test
@expected
Hello, Ann!

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
What is the difference between std::cout << x and std::cin >> x?
@rubric
screen; print; keyboard; read; input; output
@answer
std::cout << x prints x on the screen (output). std::cin >> x reads a value typed on the
keyboard and stores it in x (input). The arrows show which way the data moves.

@step practice
@title  Practice
